## Findings

**F1 — `CRIB init --help` does not show help; it performs init.** The command printed the help
header block and then immediately ran the full 5-step init (index, git hooks, `.gitattributes`
merge driver, 6 IDE MCP wirings, instruction-file adapters). Exit code was not captured (output
was piped to `head`), but the effects are visible on disk. Every other subcommand inspected
(`index`, `status`, `memory`) does gate help properly; `init` is the outlier. Someone typing
`crib init --help` to preview what it would do gets the mutation instead.

**F2 — init is not idempotent in the index, and it inflates its own input.** Post-init the tree
gains `.claude/`, `.codex/`, `.cursor/`, `.vscode/`, `.gemini/`, `.mcp.json`, `CLAUDE.md`,
`AGENTS.md`, `GEMINI.md`, `.windsurfrules`, `.github/copilot-instructions.md` and a modified
`.gitattributes`. Indexing then grows from "13 files → 109 nodes / 140 edges" to "21 files → 165
nodes / 188 edges". Those instruction files are now indexed *docs* in the graph, so the graph
contains crib's own adapter prose — which will also be returned by `query`/`brief` on this repo.
That is a plausible design choice, but it means a node/edge count is not reproducible before and
after init, and the "13 files" figure in init's own output is a pre-mutation number.

**F3 — torn-read behaviour is split-surface, and there is no drift verdict anywhere.** After a
one-word body edit with no re-index, `context --with-source` served the *new* disk text with
`grounding: "code"` and no drift/stale/needs-review field; the only change was the `hash` value
itself. After a comment edit in the doc-section above the symbol, the same response served the
*stale graph* comment in `docs[0].snippet` while serving live disk text in `source.text` — two
contradictory answers in one payload, neither flagged. A consumer that does not cache and diff the
`hash` cannot tell. The hash covers the symbol span only (proved: a comment edit inside the span
moved it, the doc-section above it did not), so the stale surface is exactly the one the hash
cannot see. This is the WP1 interest and on this evidence the protection is *detectable by a
careful consumer*, not *reported by the tool*.

**F4 — init wires the MCP server to a different binary than the one under test.** The emitted
config is `command: /Users/vishalchawla/Library/pnpm/crib serve .` — a global shim path. The CLI
under evaluation is `packages/cli/dist/cli.js`. So an IDE wired by this round's init would talk to
whatever `crib` the global shim resolves to, not to the build being validated. I did not compare
the two binaries (out of scope, and the shim may well point at the same build) but the round's
MCP surface is therefore untested here.

**F5 — the memory admission gate is real and behaves correctly in both directions.** A
`source-quote` cannot support a `convention` claim (refused, exit 1, with the admissible list
printed); a fabricated quote is refused with a message that tells the agent exactly what to do
(re-read and quote exactly). An honest quote is admitted as `grounded: 1 exact citation(s)`,
written as `active`/`recallable`, and comes back in a new process with `evidence=valid`,
`applicability=current`, `fresh=true`. This is the strongest, least ambiguous result of the round.

**F6 — generation fields are all null on a fresh repo.** `publishedGeneration`,
`readerGeneration`, `graphGeneration`, `searchGeneration` are `null`; `freshness` says
"last-known-good: never published". This is honest for a repo that has never published a
generation, but it means none of the generation-based staleness machinery is exercised by this
round and a reader cannot yet tell "no generation published" from "generation bookkeeping not
wired". `graphSourcePosition` is populated and did move across a re-index, so it is the field that
actually carries freshness information here.

**F7 — two similarly-named surfaces answer different questions.** `status.capabilities` reports
`embeddings: false` and `vector: false`, while `doctor` and `embed status` report the embedder tier
`installed / semantic-ready / multilingual-e5-large-1024-sym`. Reading them as a contradiction is
easy; the more likely reading is that `capabilities` describes *this index* (no vector channel
built) and the tier report describes *what is installed*. I did not resolve whether the naming is
intended, so I am reporting it as an ambiguity rather than a defect.

## WHAT THIS ROUND DID NOT COVER

Skipped, not run, or ambiguous — stated plainly:

1. **`gaps`, `rules`, `dossier`, `reconstruct`, `impact`, `path`, `neighbors`, `explain`, `rename`,
   `serve`, `viz`, `materialize`, `merge-driver`, `export`, `migrate-graph`, `enrich`, `rerank`,
   `sync`, `backup`, `intake`, `session bootstrap`, `handoff`, `propose`/`attest`/`audit`,
   `feedback`, `conflicts` were never invoked.** The round covered only `init`, `index`, `status`,
   `doctor`, `query`, `context`, `freshness`, `embed status`, `audit-llm`, and four `memory`
   subcommands. Most of the CLI is untested by this log.
2. **The MCP server (`crib serve`) was not exercised at all.** Every result here comes from the CLI;
   the MCP verb surface that agents actually use (`brief`, `memory_recall`, `source`, `impact`,
   `explain`, `review`, `detect_changes`) was not called. No MCP client was started.
3. **`--vectors` / `--semantic` indexing was never enabled.** Embeddings, code-vector search, the
   reranker, and any fusion of BM25 with cosine are untested. Every query result above is BM25-only.
4. **No LLM semantic layer exists on this repo** (`llmGraph: false`, 35 targets pending), so
   `audit-llm` was vacuous ("no LLM artifacts on disk"), `enrich` was not run, and no
   `hash-drift`/`needs-review` verdict on an *LLM artifact* was ever observed. The WP1 artifact
   re-verification path is therefore **not covered by this round** — I only observed the
   extracted-graph path (F3).
5. **`--dirty` / `crib update` reconciliation was not tested as a repair.** I observed the drift
   (F3) and then ran a full `crib index .`, which reconciled it; I never tested whether
   `crib update --dirty` closes the gap, nor whether the post-commit freshness hook fires (no
   commit was made in the target repo — committing was out of scope for the round).
6. **Freshness worker/service modes, the durable queue, and the generation publish path were not
   exercised** — mode stayed `manual`, worker never ran, queue was empty, nothing was ever
   published (F6).
7. **Multimodal extraction was not exercised** even though adapters reported available
   (pdf ✓, image-ocr ✓ via tesseract 5.5.2, transcribe ✗ whisper absent). No `--multimodal` run.
8. **Failure and crash paths were not deliberately induced.** No torn write, no process kill
   mid-`index`, no disk-full, no corrupted `.crib` chunk, no interrupted atomic write. The doctor's
   durability claim ("survives a PROCESS crash … power-loss durability is NOT claimed") is quoted
   from the tool's self-report and was **not independently verified** by me.
9. **`crib init --help`'s exit code was not measured** (piped to `head`), so F1's exit status is
   unknown. Every other step's exit code was captured unpiped.
10. **The target project is small (7 tracked files).** 165 nodes / 188 edges is thin: the graph is
    dominated by crib's own adapter files after init (F2). Nothing here exercises multi-package
    monorepo scoping (`--package`), clustering at scale, large blast radii, or query ranking over
    thousands of symbols. Express was listed as fallback (b) and was not needed.
11. **Only three edits were made to the target file**, all in the `/tmp` copy, all single-word
    (`index.js` L31 `'-'`→`'_'`, L23 `quadratic on input`→`REALLY_SLOW on input`, L38
    `transliteration is disabled`→`transliteration is turned off`). The round therefore does not
    cover insertions/deletions that change line numbers, whole-file rewrites, renames, or
    deletions — the cases where a span-based hash is most likely to misbehave.
12. **Timings are single-shot on a loaded machine** (load average up to 6). No repetitions, no
    variance, no cold-vs-warm separation beyond the one `query` measurement (0.439s).
13. **Ambiguous result left unresolved:** F7 (`capabilities.embeddings:false` vs `embed status:
    semantic-ready`) — I did not determine which surface a developer is meant to trust, so it is
    recorded as an ambiguity, not as a pass or a defect.

## Round metadata

- Every `crib` invocation used `HOME=/tmp/crib-e2e-20260923-020538/home`; all state (including the
  memory ledger and the embed tier manifest) lives under that temp HOME. No user state was read or
  written.
